From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: ipconfig, netsh: change the network through sg-netctl

Scripts and support tools change a Windows machine's network with
ipconfig /release and /renew and netsh; in Wine the first did nothing and
netsh was a stub. On Stained Glass OS the network is NetworkManager's, behind
sg-netctl (sg-session), which decides from the Unix account what the caller
may do (administrators and SYSTEM may change addresses).

- include/wine/sgnetctl.h: runs a native program with a temporary file as its
  output (0078) under a fixed /bin/sh that writes its exit status last -- a
  native program's process handle does not wait for it -- and parses
  sg-netctl's answer. The program and arguments are the shell's positional
  parameters, never part of its script. The file is in %TEMP%, made if an
  account that never signed in has none, else in /tmp. SG_NETCTL overrides
  the path (tests).
- ipconfig: /release and /renew [adapter, "prefix*"] for the adapters on
  DHCP, then the basic listing; /flushdns (resolvectl flush-caches).
- netsh: interface ip|ipv4 set address (static keeps the adapter's DNS, as
  on Windows; dhcp), set dns (static|dhcp), add dns (index), show
  config|address|dns; interface show interface; wlan show networks, show
  profiles, connect, disconnect. Positional and name=value parameters. A
  refusal prints Windows' elevation message and exits 1. Any other command
  is still accepted quietly, as before, so installers carry on.

Gate: test/netsh-gate.sh (a stand-in sg-netctl records what it is asked).
